    "answer": {
      "short": "$(a,b,c)=(1,1,4),\\ (1,4,1),\\ (4,1,1)$.\n\nGrouping gives $(a+b)(a+c)(b+c)=50$.",
      "worked": "Split $2abc$ into $abc+abc$ and group:\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2b+a^2c+ab^2+2abc+ac^2+b^2c+bc^2&=(a^2b+a^2c+ab^2+abc)+(abc+ac^2+b^2c+bc^2)\\\\a^2b+a^2c+ab^2+2abc+ac^2+b^2c+bc^2&=a(a+b)(b+c)+c(a+b)(b+c)\\\\a^2b+a^2c+ab^2+2abc+ac^2+b^2c+bc^2&=(a+b)(a+c)(b+c).\n\\end{align*}$$\nEach pair sum is at least $2$. The only unordered factor triple of $50$ with all factors at least $2$ is $(2,5,5)$.\nIf $a+b=2$, then $a=b=1$ and $c=4$. Assigning the sum $2$ to either of the other pairs gives the remaining permutations.\nTherefore\n$$(a,b,c)=(1,1,4),\\ (1,4,1),\\ (4,1,1).$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
